  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how host immune and genetic factors may combine to drive severe inflammatory damage after infection with a SARS‑CoV‑2 variant. At the center is an innate immune cell that senses virus through receptors such as TLRs and Fcγ receptors, including antibody-dependent activation. This cell then releases inflammatory mediators, notably IL‑8, IL‑6, and IL‑1β, suggesting an amplified cytokine and chemokine response.\n\nAround this core, several labeled genetic associations appear to shape disease: MHC class II and SOCS1 are linked to CD4+ T-cell responses, while MHC class I is linked to CD8+ T-cell interactions with endothelial cells. The diagram implies that altered signaling pathways and antigen presentation can skew T-cell activation, leading to production of IFNγ and IL‑17A and to increased cytotoxicity.\n\nA second major theme is abnormal humoral immunity. Dysregulated B cells produce autoantibodies and antimicrobial antibodies, which can trigger Fcγ receptor signaling and complement activation. These antibodies appear to contribute directly to endothelial injury, shown as autoantibody-mediated cell damage along the vessel lining. A possible superantigen-like effect is also indicated near T-cell–endothelial interactions. Overall, the image portrays severe disease as a convergence of viral triggers, immune dysregulation, genetic susceptibility, and vascular damage.",
